<!-- Copyright (c) 2026 Elliot Cartee. -->
<!-- This renderer supplies English interface labels through en-US only.
     Keep authored worksheet prose in British English. -->
<!-- Workshop 8 covers Week 9 videos 4 and 5 (powers of sine and cosine,
     sine and cosine of a complex number) and Week 10 videos 1-2, probably
     3 (eigenvalues and eigenvectors). Week 9 video 2 (multiple-angle
     formulae) was planned too, but its exercise was cut for time on
     24 Nov 2026. Video 3 (trace, determinant and the eigenvalues of A^m)
     may not have been seen, so nothing here needs it: Exercise 5(a)
     derives the trace and determinant form of the characteristic equation
     from video 2, and Exercises 6 and 7 use only A v = lambda v.
     Notation follows the slides: j, cos(theta) = (e^{j theta} + e^{-j theta})/2,
     A v = lambda v with v not zero, the characteristic equation
     det(A - lambda I) = 0. As on every sheet, vectors have arrows and
     matrices square brackets, where the slides use bold and round brackets.
     Elliot prefers sin(theta) and ln(x) with parentheses.
     One matrix runs through the sheet: A in Exercise 1 returns in the
     challenge.
     Plan: about 34 minutes of core work, counting discussion parts double,
     plus an optional challenge. Each page heading names the highest Bloom
     level that page reaches; the warm-up stays at Understand. -->
<pretext xml:lang="en-US" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <xi:include href="docinfo.ptx"/>
  <article xml:id="worksheet08wks">
    <title>Engineering Maths 1</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>University of Bristol — EMAT10100</p>
      <p>Use the printer icon for a copy with working space. Give exact answers unless stated otherwise.</p>
    </introduction>
    <worksheet>
      <title>Workshop 8: Complex trigonometry and eigenvalues</title>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Warm-up</title>
          <p>Please work in collaboration with your classmates to complete the following exercises. This means sharing ideas and asking each other questions.</p>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Understand (Week 10 video 1), straight from the definition, as
             the video tries out vectors. Two of the four vectors belong to
             the same eigenvalue, so the point that any non-zero multiple of
             an eigenvector is an eigenvector comes up without being asked.
             A returns in the challenge. The "-twocol" id sets the tasks two
             to a row (assets/custom.css), so each vector has its own
             space. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-eigencheck-twocol">
          <introduction>
            <p>Let <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}4 \amp 1\\2 \amp 3\end{bmatrix}</m>. For each vector below, decide whether it is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>, and if so, give its eigenvalue.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p><m>\colvec{1\\1}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p><m>\colvec{2\\1}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p><m>\colvec{1\\-2}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.0in">
            <statement><p><m>\colvec{-3\\-3}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
        <!-- Understand (Week 10 video 2): why the definition requires v to
             be non-zero. The zero vector is written as a column, because
             5 written next to an arrowed 0 reads like an arrowed 50. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-zero" workspace="1.4in">
          <statement>
            <p>A student adds <m>\colvec{0\\0}</m> to the list in Exercise 1, because <m>A\colvec{0\\0}=5\colvec{0\\0}</m>. Explain in your own words why the zero vector is never counted as an eigenvector.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Practice: Powers of Sine and Cosine</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, two ways (Week 9 video 4). The lecture expands sin^3 and
             cos^5; this mixed product is new. Part (b) also needs
             sin^2 x = (1 - cos(2x))/2, which video 4's motivation slide
             gives. The exponential forms are stated here because Exercise 4
             can use them too. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-powers">
          <introduction>
            <p>Write <m>\sin^2(\theta)\cos^2(\theta)</m> as a constant plus multiples of <m>\cos(k\theta)</m>, in two different ways.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.6in">
            <statement><p>Use <m>\cos(\theta)=\tfrac{1}{2}\left(e^{j\theta}+e^{-j\theta}\right)</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)=\tfrac{1}{2j}\left(e^{j\theta}-e^{-j\theta}\right)</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Use <m>\sin(2\theta)=2\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta)</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Evaluate: Cosine of a Complex Number</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Evaluate (Week 9 video 5). Part (a) needs
             cos(jx) = cosh(x), from the formula sheet or from the
             exponential forms in Exercise 3, and cosh(ln(2)) works out as
             Workshop 7's cosh(ln(3)) did. Part (b) is about the students'
             own answer to (a). Video 5 raises the same point before solving
             cos(z) = 2. Merged from two exercises on 24 Nov 2026, when
             sin(j ln(3)) was cut for time. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-complex-cos">
          <introduction>
            <p>A student says that <m>\lvert\cos(z)\rvert\le 1</m> for every complex number <m>z</m>, because the graph of cosine stays between <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.8in">
            <statement><p>Evaluate <m>\cos(j\ln(2))</m> exactly.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.2in">
            <statement><p>Do you agree with the student? Explain your answer.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Create: Your Own Eigenvalues</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, Create, then Apply (Week 10 video 2). Part (a) is the
             trace and determinant form of the characteristic equation,
             derived here because video 3 may not have been seen. Part (b)
             uses part (a) in reverse; "no zero entries" rules out a
             triangular answer, so the neighbour has to solve the quadratic.
             Part (c) is the one place on the sheet where students solve
             (A - lambda I) v = 0. Every pair makes a different problem for
             each other, and different eigenvectors for the same eigenvalue
             can both be right. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-create">
          <introduction>
            <p>Work with a neighbour on this exercise. If you are working alone, find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of your own matrix in part (c).</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.3in">
            <statement><p>Let <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}a \amp b\\c \amp d\end{bmatrix}</m>. Show that the characteristic equation <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m> can be written as <m>\lambda^2-(a+d)\lambda+(ad-bc)=0</m> for any numbers <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> and <m>d</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement><p>Choose two different whole numbers and keep them to yourself. Use part (a) to write down a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix with no zero entries whose eigenvalues are your two numbers.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.2in">
            <statement><p>Swap matrices with a neighbour. Find the eigenvalues of their matrix, and an eigenvector for each eigenvalue. Then swap back and check each other's eigenvectors.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Practice: Powers of a Matrix</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Apply, then Understand (Week 10 video 2). Only the definition
             is needed, so this works whether or not video 3's rule for the
             eigenvalues of A^m has been seen: part (b), added at Elliot's
             request on 24 Nov 2026, is the step M(Mv) = M(2v) = 2Mv, which
             the challenge also needs. The power 2026 (Elliot's idea,
             24 Nov 2026) makes multiplying out hopeless, even on a
             calculator, so it replaces an instruction not to; change the
             year if the sheet is reused. The challenge builds on this
             idea. -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-matrix-powers">
          <introduction>
            <p>Let
            <me>M=\begin{bmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp 5\\1 \amp 3 \amp -2\\0 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{bmatrix},\quad \vec{v}=\colvec{1\\-1\\0}.</me>
            </p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement><p>Show that <m>\vec{v}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>M</m>, and find its eigenvalue.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.4in">
            <statement><p>Use part (a) to find <m>M^2\vec{v}</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.9in">
            <statement><p>Find <m>M^{2026}\vec{v}</m>. Explain your reasoning.</p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Challenge: The Largest Eigenvalue Wins</title>
        </assemblage>
        <!-- Deep (adapted from Elliot's A&M linear algebra problem 6.1 #10,
             with Exercise 1's matrix). Part (a) is a 2 by 2 linear system
             (Week 4). The conclusion's claim is true: the original
             PageRank paper computes the dominant eigenvector by repeated
             multiplication (the power method). -->
        <exercise xml:id="w8-challenge">
          <introduction>
            <p>The matrix <m>A</m> from Exercise 1 has eigenvectors <m>\colvec{1\\1}</m> with eigenvalue <m>5</m> and <m>\colvec{1\\-2}</m> with eigenvalue <m>2</m>. Let <m>\vec{v}=\colvec{2\\-1}</m>.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.2in">
            <statement><p>Write <m>\vec{v}</m> as a sum of multiples of the two eigenvectors.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.7in">
            <statement><p>Find a formula for <m>A^m\vec{v}</m>, and check it when <m>m=1</m>.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.3in">
            <statement><p>Explain in your own words why <m>A^m\vec{v}</m> points almost along <m>\colvec{1\\1}</m> when <m>m</m> is large.</p></statement>
          </task>
          <conclusion>
            <p>Google's original PageRank ranked web pages with this idea. Multiplying by one huge matrix again and again picks out the eigenvector with the largest eigenvalue.</p>
          </conclusion>
        </exercise>
      </page>
    </worksheet>
  </article>
</pretext>
